[Music]  [Music]  hey guys pete here can you believe that  there are only two more episodes of  better call saul left today i'll be  previewing what we might expect to see  in episode 12 which is titled waterworks  the teaser that aired mid-credit seems  so connected to what happened at the end  of episode 11 that it's almost  suspicious most of what we've gotten in  that department after the mid-season  break has been on a scale from  mysterious to misleading so we'll look  at some alternative ideas of what might  be going on there there are no promo  photos this week and that and a lot of  context clues lead me to believe my  theory about seeing some kim this week  is probably correct i've been racking my  brain since we did the live stream on  how that might work so i'll be giving  you an update on that as well if any of  what i just mentioned sounds like  spoilers to you then this is officially  your chance to leave and with that out  of the way let's get into it  series co-creator vince gilligan will do  something he's never done before this  week write and direct an episode of  better call saul he did do this on  breaking bad of course he was the  creator and showrunner of that series  and he co-wrote and directed a few  episodes of the prequel including the  pilot that he directed and co-wrote with  show runner peter gould but this is the  first time that he has both credits for  bcs talk about the end of an era i guess  if you've been saving up an ernest bravo  vince this will be your week to unleash  it waterworks has a short and vague  description that reads the stakes are  raised when a discovery is made as has  been the case with most of the second  half of the season there's no telling  exactly what this is referring to if you  think about gene he has just broken into  the home of the mark that he learned had  cancer a lot of people think he might  discover his dead body due to medication  interactions remember part of the scam  involves knocking the person out with  barbiturates so they can steal their  identities while they sleep i suppose if  those reacted with his cancer meds then  that would raise the stakes there's a  much repeated theory that i saw in the  comments in my last video that gene will  find him as he's dying similar to how  walt found jane and then he'll have to  make a decision of whether he saves him  or just goes on with the robbery while  this would put him in an interesting  situation i'm not sure that's where  things are heading part of that is  related to a new york times interview  that i'll come back to but be warned  that just serves to make things more  confusing rather than clearing anything  up let's talk about the teaser where at  least what's happening is pretty  straightforward two police officers have  arrived at a home and they exit their  car unlike the previous teasers we don't  get a line of dialogue from gene or any  other character instead we just hear the  police radio saying that backup is on  route and for the officers to proceed  with caution apparently a reddit user  discovered that the police radio was  recycled from a scene from season five  so i wouldn't think too hard about it  it's clear they're responding to a call  and i guess the important questions are  is this the house gene just broke into  and is he still inside i'm guessing the  marketing department would like for us  to believe that but how would that  actually play out  the street looks like it could be the  same as where jeff dropped him off and  one thing that stands out is that one of  the officers is putting his hand on his  gun at the end we know that a break-in  occurred at the cancer man's house and  maybe they just aren't sure if there's  someone still inside gene told jeff to  come pick him up in 20 minutes what type  of scenario would see the police get  there before he does or what would keep  gene inside for longer than that you  could go in a lot of different  directions trying to answer these  questions which i definitely have but  either way it doesn't feel like he would  actually be caught here they made a  point of showing marion seeing him  arrive late at night so it's clear that  she has an idea that something's going  on i'm not sure why you would set that  up if he were just going to get arrested  after he leaves also she's got that  computer that he showed her how to use  if she were to make a discovery about  who he really is i would say that that  would also raise the stakes i also think  that we've seen several points where his  charm isn't working as well as he thinks  it is and that hints again that she  knows more than what she's letting on  you had him stumble with the question  about nippy when he forgot about his own  lie in the first episode she was in  there was the reaction she had when he  took jeff outside to have their drinks  and so by the time that she sees him  coming back to the house late at night  she probably has some suspicions about  him in a roundabout way this would feel  like it was justice for irene landry  that was one of his most despicable acts  where he used this same charm that is so  effective on the elderly and that makes  the prospects of him underestimating  marian and her turning things around all  the more tantalizing again you could go  in a lot of different directions to try  to make that make sense and just to  complicate things further there was this  new york times article that came out  before the season started in that bob  odenkirk was being interviewed while he  was filming this episode with vince  gilligan it describes him sneaking  around someone's home picking up  different items which would make sense  for what happened at the end of episode  11. the problem is that one of the shots  they describe has jean looking down from  a balcony and the cancer guy appears to  live in a single level ranch-style house  that would seem to indicate that he's  still out there breaking into other  houses after what happened with him i  guess if i was pressed to make a general  prediction i would say that i think he  gets out of the break-in situation only  to learn that he has an even bigger  problem to deal with the articles writer  also describes a cat and mouse game  going on with a different character  inside this house while he's drinking a  glass of liquor so as i mentioned before  it's more confusing than anything else  if you think about the ending of the  last episode  and the way they cut it together with  him approaching walt's school you're  watching two decisions that alter the  trajectory of his life in both cases he  does something he shouldn't do because  he can't help himself if we look at his  situation as an addiction he's in the  height of it in the breaking bad  timeline and in relapse in omaha we know  the phone call is the event that set off  his most recent episode and my gut  reaction when i was watching the episode  for the first time was that they were  keeping the contents of that from us so  they could reveal it later it just  didn't really make sense for them to do  that otherwise and tom schnauz did  confirm that we would hear the details  of this call in a future episode to the  hollywood reporter if you go looking  you'll find several people who cleaned  up this audio and while there's some  debate on exactly what's being said the  one thing they all agree on is that he's  talking to kim and that he addresses her  by name the first thing i thought about  was that they were doing the same thing  they did with nachos call to mike in  episode 602. in the next episode we got  to see the call from his perspective and  i think we'll get the same thing here  with kim this is not some kind of far  out idea i'm definitely not alone in  this thinking it seems to be popular to  the point that it's almost inevitable  the episode's title waterworks will  certainly have more than one  interpretation but there's an obvious  connection to her working at a sprinkler  company i don't think it's a question of  if kim will appear but more of how much  of her life will get to see that will  depend on her situation and i think  that's the most interesting part of this  if she's just been living her life  without incident since she made the  decision to leave the marriage behind i  suppose we wouldn't need a lot to  explain that things are basically the  same show some scenes with her day to  day maybe even do another split screen  montage and we know enough about the  character to piece things together i got  the impression that she was shifting the  responsibility and on some level the  blame to herself in their last  conversation and i don't think that  bodes well for an easy transition to a  happy carefree life she may have taken a  page out of howard's book though and  actually dealt with her problems  depending on where she's at in that  journey or if she's on it at all would  dictate how much we'd need to see to  understand what's going on with her  based on jean's reaction to that call  you could make that work she knows what  happened she told him to take care of  the situation and turn himself in and  then told him not to contact her could  that work as a satisfying ending i mean  i guess on some level jimmy might think  that she can save him or that getting  back together would take him out of the  spiral he's currently in he would be  wrong but maybe being deprived of that  connection would force him to go in one  direction or the other from what we've  seen lately that would probably be  complete self-destruction that's kind of  where we're at but i suppose it's  possible that he tries to reach out  again and through that realizes that  there really is no chance and that is  what's difficult for someone who cares  about somebody who is spiraling an  addiction but there's not much you can  do when the person is engaged in the  compulsion her doing what she thought  was right would be an ending for the  character but i'm expecting it to get  more complicated than that we also need  to learn if they've been in contact  since she's left my initial thinking was  that if he knew where she worked then  they must have been in contact at some  point in the six years since she left  they were married so it could be as  simple as them needing to get a divorce  not knowing if that's true or how things  went down makes it hard to imagine what  their ending will be and so i don't see  how we don't have a decent amount of her  life in the upcoming episode and that's  not even to mention the dramatic effect  of bringing her back after being gone  for two episodes or the possibility of  having her showing up without the  ponytail at the end of the last  installment things felt darker which  makes it seem like we might be running  out of road for him to redeem himself  even a little bit it's also not clear  where the inspiration could come from i  think marion will play a role in the  current scamming coming to an end and  beyond her and jeff who else is left  maybe the cancer man they spent some  time letting us get to know him and he  turned out to be quite sympathetic could  he see past this mask and in his  position with nothing to lose get  through to jimmy i keep going back to  the last episode and wondering about  what they were trying to tell us as far  as his current situation we saw a lot of  detail about the progression of his  scams the slippery slope that will  always lead back to him being saul  goodman or maybe worse and him not being  able to stop in relation to walt and  jesse's appearance we learned that saul  could have left them alone and got solid  advice from mike to do so but ignored it  there's an added layer there of mike not  being able to take his own advice later  on in the story but i don't suppose  that's very important for what we're  talking about right now what stands out  is that him making the decision led to  some discussion about whether saul was  responsible for walter white's rise to  power and from my perspective i feel  that that underestimates what a force of  nature that character is what's more  interesting i think is how jimmy feels  about it because going in i felt that if  they were gonna bring these two back for  that to work it should be more about him  in our live stream discussion on tuesday  we talked about this and two things  stuck with me first gill pointed out  that from saul's perspective he could  believe that he was responsible for the  carnage that walter white left in his  path the second was courtney bringing up  all the frankenstein references and i've  seen some other interesting takes on  that in the past couple of days as well  a simple explanation of what that story  is about is that victor with a c  frankenstein creates a man from pieces  of corpses and brings a brand new  creature to life initially it had good  intentions and sought affection but  everyone it met was afraid of him  because of course he was a bunch of  different pieces of bodies put together  and brought back to life it's kind of  terrifying he eventually turns on the  doctor who never took responsibility for  what he created with those things in  mind and having no idea how kim might  find herself compelled to intervene in  the current situation i wonder if the  same way jimmy thinks he created a  monster in walt kim might think she  created a monster in seoul her  encouraging his transformation was a big  part of the beginning of this season i  imagine there would have to be some  other steps that we haven't seen maybe  she knew what he was up to during the  events of breaking bad or maybe  something related to the phone call as  mentioned before i do think we'll see  that from her perspective in this  episode but either way i think this  makes more sense than her coming out of  retirement to defend him in court or  just deciding to rescue him based on  their past relationship at the very  least it's a way in and those seem to be  in short supply at the end of episode  611. maybe it's just me trying to cope  with the reality that's being laid out  in front of me but i defer back to peter  gould's assessment of how the two main  characters of the series are different  he said if there were a simple way to  distinguish the two characters i think  walt wants to be respected and feared  and jimmy i think really wants everyone  to like him he wants love and i think  that starting point has made his decline  much more tragic and i think it's  natural to want to see some type of  redemption although gould did add that  he goes about it in all kinds of strange  ways and i can't argue with that i've  seen some people bringing back the chuck  was right all along argument which i  think overlooks his complicity and  making his own prophecy come true i  wouldn't say chuck was responsible for  saul any more than i would say that he's  responsible for walter white but i think  it's interesting to think about why one  brother is a lot more sympathetic than  the other while chuck was mentally ill  and that's a big part of what was  happening with him during the series he  was also always convinced that his  brother was beyond redemption like  walter he never saw what he was doing  was wrong and in the end he was just  bitter that he didn't win that's part of  why a crash and burn finale would be  depressing thinking that jimmy  internalized that and went out thinking  chuck was right about him this is a  universe that likes to explore the  consequences of actions though we only  have two more episodes to go and i think  we need to find out how kim fits into  this ending so yeah bring that on and i  think that's a great place to leave  things let me know in the comments what  you're expecting to see there are two  more scenes with walt and jesse  appearing separately according to brian  cranston so what are you hoping to see  with those please like this video if you  enjoyed it please subscribe to my  channel if you haven't already and  thanks for watching i'll talk to you  soon

